WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:05.700
Guillermo del Toro's Pinocchio is a&nbsp;
stunning stop-motion adaptation of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:09.120
the Italian children's story about a&nbsp;
wooden puppet who becomes a real boy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:12.240
but how does it compare to&nbsp;
Disney's animated classic?
&nbsp;

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:16.560
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, I'm Jan and&nbsp;
in this video I'm explaining the biggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:21.900
differences between Disney's iconic movie and&nbsp;
del Toro's magical take on the legendary tale.
&nbsp;

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:24.720
Spoilers ahead, so take care&nbsp;
if you haven't seen it yet.
&nbsp;

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:30.300
While the star of Disney's film is crafted&nbsp;
for maximum cuteness, with rounded features,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:35.160
big eyes, rosy cheeks, and bright clothes,
Guillermo del Toro took inspiration from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:40.320
darkly humorous illustrations of artist Gris&nbsp;
Grimly for his Pinocchio, which means he's all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:45.480
spindly limbs and stripped right back to his&nbsp;
more realistically grainy wooden elements.
&nbsp;

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:51.300
And unlike Disney's Geppetto who lovingly paints&nbsp;
and names the puppet, a drunken carving session&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:56.580
by del Toro's Geppetto means the marionette&nbsp;
has a rough and ready look with twigs, tape,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.580 --> 00:01:00.840
and nails sticking out, which fits with the&nbsp;
film's theme of embracing imperfection.
&nbsp;

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:06.000
But del Toro and his fellow filmmakers&nbsp;
haven't just revamped Pinocchio's appearance;
&nbsp;

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:11.460
they've also put a completely new spin on&nbsp;
Carlo Collodi's 19th-century children's story.
&nbsp;

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:16.560
Whereas Disney follows the original Italian&nbsp;
tale and eventually turns the wooden puppet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:21.900
into a real, flesh-and-blood boy, del Toro takes&nbsp;
things in a different direction as he says he's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:25.320
"never believed that transformation&nbsp;
[should] be demanded to gain love".
&nbsp;

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:31.500
Gone too is the idea that obedience and conformity&nbsp;
are necessarily always a thing to aspire to,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:37.320
with the Mexican Oscar-winner showing instead how&nbsp;
disobedience can actually be a positive thing.
&nbsp;

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:42.420
And as del Toro notes, in this version,&nbsp;
'Everybody learns from [Pinocchio]', who's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:47.400
winningly voiced by Gregory Mann, rather than&nbsp;
just Pinocchio learning from everybody else.
&nbsp;

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:52.080
The talking Cricket who pops up just&nbsp;
a handful of times in Collodi's book,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:56.640
where he actually gets killed by Pinocchio,&nbsp;
was given a much-expanded role by Disney.
&nbsp;

00:01:56.640 --> 00:02:02.040
As well as being Pinocchio's conscience and&nbsp;
providing much humour, Jiminy Cricket also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:07.080
bookends the 1940 animation and sings&nbsp;
the iconic When You Wish Upon A Star. 
&nbsp;

00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:12.960
Although del Toro's Sebastian J. Cricket does&nbsp;
open and close the new film, adds a lot of comedy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:18.720
and even gets to sing an enjoyably jazzy musical&nbsp;
number, you won't find him wearing fancy clothes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:19.620
like Jiminy...
Solid gold too.
&nbsp;

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:25.500
...as the filmmakers have taken back his&nbsp;
look back more to his pointy, insect roots,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:30.000
albeit with some intriguing bluey-purple&nbsp;
colouring and moustache-like mandibles.
&nbsp;

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:33.420
Sebastian, who's entertainingly&nbsp;
voiced by Ewan McGregor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:37.140
is also more of a philosopher and&nbsp;
raconteur than his Disney counterpart
&nbsp;

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:40.440
I'm a novelist, currently&nbsp;
immersed in writing my memoirs.
&nbsp;

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:44.700
though he finds he learns as much from&nbsp;
Pinocchio as the marionette learns from him.
&nbsp;

00:02:44.700 --> 00:02:48.120
Well I tried my best, and&nbsp;
that's the best anyone can do.
&nbsp;

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:52.800
Pinocchio taught me that.
Disney's Geppetto is a loving, playful, and cuddly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:58.800
character with two charming animal sidekicks:
a cute cat with an attitude called Figaro and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:04.320
an eye-lash-batting goldfish named Cleo.
Del Toro's Geppetto is much more angular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:09.780
and gnarled and instead of adorable companion&nbsp;
creatures, the old woodcarver gets a human son&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:15.060
called Carlo as well as a heart-breaking backstory&nbsp;
for why he creates Pinocchio in the first place. 
&nbsp;

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:20.460
In fact, Game of Thrones star David Bradley&nbsp;
who voices Geppetto sees it as a complex,
&nbsp;

00:03:20.460 --> 00:03:25.080
'real emotional roller coaster of a part
– more like King Lear than the Pinocchio&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:29.160
[he] remembered [as a kid].'
Disney adapted the original book's Fox&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:34.680
and Cat duo who were murderous robbers into&nbsp;
con-men Honest John and Gideon, who bring a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:40.740
barrelful of scheming slapstick fun to the film.
But with the exception of the magical Pinocchio,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.740 --> 00:03:44.640
Cricket, and Wood Sprite, del Toro&nbsp;
wanted other elements of his version&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:49.500
to feel closer to the real world.
So, instead of a talking cat and fox,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:55.200
he created two new characters, Count Volpe&nbsp;
and Spazzatura, who channel both the original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.200 --> 00:04:01.500
animals as well as the Puppet Master Stromboli&nbsp;
aka Mangiafuoco from the animation and book.
&nbsp;

00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:08.220
Volpe, whose name is Italian for fox, is a human&nbsp;
ringmaster who runs a travelling carnival and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.220 --> 00:04:13.380
lures Pinocchio into starring in his show.
Both del Toro and Disney's vulpine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.380 --> 00:04:19.080
characters are melodramatic, actorly types.
Can you not see how desperate the situation is?
&nbsp;

00:04:19.080 --> 00:04:25.440
I'm speaking, my boy, of the theatre. 
And Volpe's perfectly voiced by Christoph Waltz.
&nbsp;

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:30.900
I love how the character's fox-inspired name and&nbsp;
tricksy nature are reflected in his appearance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:35.580
from his long, reddish nose, to his&nbsp;
orange-brown coat with white fur collar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.580 --> 00:04:39.720
and his ginger hairstyle that shoots&nbsp;
up like fox ears or devil horns. 
&nbsp;

00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:44.700
And then there's Volpe's side-kick, an&nbsp;
often-mistreated monkey called Spazzatura,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.700 --> 00:04:50.580
who apart from vocalisations by Cate Blanchett,&nbsp;
speaks only via some marionettes, though the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.580 --> 00:04:55.740
character does add plenty of physical comedy to&nbsp;
the film, similar to Disney's mostly silent Cat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.740 --> 00:05:01.740
and it even has some poignant moments as well.
In a big change from Disney's Blue Fairy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.740 --> 00:05:07.020
and the book's Fairy with Turquoise Hair,&nbsp;
del Toro's film instead gives us two ethereal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.020 --> 00:05:12.360
blue sister spirits called the Wood Sprite&nbsp;
and Death, both voiced by Tilda Swinton.
&nbsp;

00:05:12.360 --> 00:05:14.520
Who on earth are you?
A guardian.
&nbsp;

00:05:14.520 --> 00:05:19.740
I care for the little things, the&nbsp;
forgotten things, the lost ones.
&nbsp;

00:05:19.740 --> 00:05:25.080
The Wood Sprite has huge feathery wings covered&nbsp;
with blinking eyes which was inspired by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.080 --> 00:05:30.480
medieval Mexican painting of angels and is also a&nbsp;
deliberate nod to another of del Toro's creations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:35.460
the Angel of Death from Hellboy.
With its darker themes, del Toro's movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.460 --> 00:05:41.100
isn't afraid to introduce the concept of Death,&nbsp;
though it's done in a darkly amusing manner.
&nbsp;

00:05:41.100 --> 00:05:46.500
For example, the odd group of rabbits who work in&nbsp;
the Underworld seem more interested in their game&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.500 --> 00:05:50.700
of cards than making sure the people inside&nbsp;
the coffins they carry are actually dead.
&nbsp;

00:05:50.700 --> 00:05:55.740
And the whole scene is a nod to the famous&nbsp;
series of paintings "Dogs Playing Poker".
&nbsp;

00:05:55.740 --> 00:06:00.540
Death herself is a sphinx-like creature&nbsp;
with horns and wings covered in eyes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.540 --> 00:06:05.940
and a forked tail with snakes at the end.
Whereas Disney's Blue Fairy ultimately turns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.940 --> 00:06:10.680
Pinocchio from a wooden puppet into a real boy&nbsp;
made of flesh and bone, in del Toro's version&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.680 --> 00:06:15.540
although Death does tell Pinocchio he can choose&nbsp;
to change from an immortal being into a mortal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.540 --> 00:06:19.920
one, he's still very much made of wood.
The idea behind this change was that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.920 --> 00:06:24.840
according to del Toro, 'You don't have to change&nbsp;
who you are to be loved', but at the same time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.840 --> 00:06:31.140
Pinocchio's new-found mortality also shows that&nbsp;
death is crucial to give life meaning and value.
&nbsp;

00:06:31.140 --> 00:06:35.160
The one thing that makes human life&nbsp;
precious and meaningful you see...
&nbsp;

00:06:35.160 --> 00:06:39.300
is how brief it is.
A horrifyingly memorable part of Disney's movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.300 --> 00:06:45.420
is Pleasure Island, where the malevolent Coachman&nbsp;
takes disobedient boys to turn them into donkeys.
&nbsp;

00:06:45.420 --> 00:06:51.420
In place of that, del Toro's film, which is set&nbsp;
in 1930s Italy, has young boys being sent to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.420 --> 00:06:56.520
fascist training camp run by the Podesta,&nbsp;
voiced by del Toro regular Ron Perlman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.520 --> 00:07:00.780
and there they're turned into killers to&nbsp;
fight for Mussolini and the Fatherland.
&nbsp;

00:07:00.780 --> 00:07:06.420
The Podesta's son Candlewick, who's voiced by Finn&nbsp;
Wolfhard from Stranger Things, is the equivalent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.420 --> 00:07:11.340
of Disney's naughty Lampwick boy who befriends&nbsp;
Pinocchio on the way to Pleasure Island.
&nbsp;

00:07:11.340 --> 00:07:16.680
Although he initially bullies Pinocchio,&nbsp;
del Toro's Candlewick later becomes a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.680 --> 00:07:20.280
good friend and frees himself from&nbsp;
his father's oppressive world view,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.280 --> 00:07:25.980
and so avoids a horrible fate like that suffered&nbsp;
by his fairy tale and Disney counterparts.
&nbsp;

00:07:25.980 --> 00:07:31.380
Candlewick's disobedience to his fascist father&nbsp;
and the regime is part of del Toro's emphasis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.380 --> 00:07:36.660
on the virtues of disobedience, similar to&nbsp;
the joyous way that Pinocchio pokes fun at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.660 --> 00:07:41.940
the Italian dictator Mussolini at the carnival,&nbsp;
when Il Duce turns up to watch a performance.
&nbsp;

00:07:41.940 --> 00:07:44.580
These puppets, I do not like.
Shoot him!
&nbsp;

00:07:44.580 --> 00:07:48.960
When it comes to its soundtrack, del&nbsp;
Toro's movie has a steep climb to reach&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.960 --> 00:07:53.400
the dizzy heights of the Oscar-winning,&nbsp;
much-loved songs of the 1940 movie,
&nbsp;

00:07:53.400 --> 00:07:58.440
from When You Wish Upon A Star, which not&nbsp;
only won an Academy Award but became Disney's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.440 --> 00:08:03.900
signature tune, to Jiminy Cricket's jaunty Give A&nbsp;
Little Whistle, Disney's first-ever villain song&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.900 --> 00:08:08.880
Hi-Diddle-Dee-Dee (An Actor's Life for Me),&nbsp;
and Pinocchio's catchy I've Got No Strings.
&nbsp;

00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:12.750
So, comparing Disney and del&nbsp;
Toro's songs is almost impossible
&nbsp;

00:08:12.750 --> 00:08:17.760
– after all, we've heard Disney's tracks&nbsp;
for decades now while del Toro's tunes are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.760 --> 00:08:21.720
receiving their very first airing.
Still, I did enjoy the new songs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.720 --> 00:08:26.880
and think they work well in their context.
Look out for the sweet melancholy of Geppetto's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.880 --> 00:08:32.340
lullaby 'My Son', Pinocchio's amusing 'Everything&nbsp;
Is New to Me' and the touching 'Ciao Papa',&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.340 --> 00:08:37.560
as well as Volpe's 'We Were A King Once'&nbsp;
which has real vibes of Fagin's songs You've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.560 --> 00:08:41.340
Got to Pick A Pocket or Two and Reviewing&nbsp;
the Situation from the musical Oliver!
&nbsp;

00:08:41.340 --> 00:08:45.960
Viewed by many, including del Toro,&nbsp;
as a pinnacle of Disney's animation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.960 --> 00:08:51.180
the studio's second ever animated feature is&nbsp;
still as gorgeous as ever, with its dazzling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.180 --> 00:08:56.340
hand-drawn 2D imagery coupled with, what&nbsp;
was for the time, ground-breaking effects.
&nbsp;

00:08:56.340 --> 00:09:01.320
Meanwhile, del Toro and his talented team&nbsp;
bring an incredible eye for detail to their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.320 --> 00:09:05.940
new movie which combines rich visuals&nbsp;
with both macabre and moving moments.
&nbsp;

00:09:05.940 --> 00:09:10.800
And I do love the idea of using stop-motion&nbsp;
puppets to tell the famous tale,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.800 --> 00:09:16.020
which fits so well with their particular&nbsp;
spin that wooden boy Pinocchio often acts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.020 --> 00:09:20.220
more like a real human than many of the&nbsp;
actual human characters in the story.
&nbsp;

00:09:20.220 --> 00:09:24.120
So, what do you think of del Toro's film?
And what's your favourite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.120 --> 00:09:27.780
moment in either Pinocchio?
Let me know in the comments below.
